  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough continues to move northeast, keeping our area \nunder southwest flow aloft. Shortwave impulses embedded in the flow \ncombined with abundant moisture maintains medium to high (50-80%) \nPoPs across southeast New Mexico and portions of far west Texas \nSunday night. By Monday, chances of showers/storms become more \nwidespread as another shortwave develops overhead and shifts east. \nSoutheasterly upslope flow provides orographic lift, yielding \ncontinued chances for convection across the higher terrain. \nLocalized flash flooding will be possible in areas where training \nstorms occur.\n\nThe shortwave begins to lift northeast on Tuesday and weak ridging \ndevelops behind it. As the shortwave departs, low (10-20%) chances \nof rain are maintained across the eastern third of our CWA. By \nWednesday, ridging builds slightly and brings drier conditions. \nHowever, this is short-lived as long-range model guidance shows \ntoughing with difluent flow aloft late next week. If this verifies, \nmuch of the area may see more showers and storms. We will continue \nto monitor trends and adjust the forecast as details become more \nclear.\n  \nGreening",
